        {
          "id": 20,
          "type": "text",
          "title": "How to read this dashboard",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "gridPos": {
            "h": 11,
            "w": 24,
            "x": 0,
            "y": 47
          },
          "options": {
            "mode": "markdown",
            "code": {
              "language": "plaintext",
              "showLineNumbers": false,
              "showMiniMap": false
            },
            "content": "## Reading these numbers\n\n**Every pod reports the same figures.** The plugin queries the Redmine database, so each replica answers `/metrics` with identical, installation-wide values. Every panel here therefore aggregates with `max`, never `sum` — `sum` would multiply everything by the number of pods.\n\n**\"Active\" means a browser request.** It comes from Redmine's own session tokens, whose `updated_on` core bumps on every request (at most once a minute). API-key calls and the mail fetcher create no session token and are invisible here — this is \"who would notice a restart\", not \"what load is the box under\".\n\n**Sessions outnumber active users.** A session token stays valid until it expires, so somebody who logged in this morning still counts as a session long after they stopped clicking.\n\n**The mail metrics are counters.** Use `rate()` or `increase()`, as the panels above do. `redmine_helpdesk_mails_total` only exists when [redmine_expert_helpdesk](https://github.com/expertZentrale/redmine_expert_helpdesk) is installed; those panels stay empty otherwise. A notification with an empty `project` label is an account or security mail.\n\nMetric reference: [redmine_expert_metrics README](https://github.com/expertZentrale/redmine_expert_metrics#readme)."
          },
          "transparent": false,
          "pluginVersion": "11.0.0"
        }
      ]
    }
